WEBVTT
TITLE Antichains, chain covers and the width of a poset: Dilworth, Mirsky, Sperner and the matching that computes them — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Five tasks, four constraints: A before B, A before C, and both B and C before D. E waits for nobody.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
A chain is a set in which every pair is comparable: A, B, D is a chain of three.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.000
An antichain is a set in which no two distinct elements are comparable: B, C, E is one of size three.

4
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.000
A chain and an antichain share at most one element. That one fact carries both theorems.

5
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:40.000
The width is the size of the largest antichain. Here it is three, and B, C, E is one of them.

6
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:48.000
The height is the size of the longest chain. Here it is also three, and that is a coincidence.

7
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:56.000
A chain of four has width one and height four. An antichain of four has width four and height one.

8
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:04.000
For a poset of n elements, width times height is at least n. That is a real inequality, and it is not an equality.

9
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Give each element its level: one plus the longest chain that ends at it.

10
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Elements of the same level are never comparable, so each level is an antichain.

11
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Three antichains here, and the height is three: a partition into three antichains, and no fewer will do.

12
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Mirsky: the minimum number of antichains covering a poset equals its height.

13
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Now cover the same poset with chains instead. A B D is one chain; C is another; E is a third.

14
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Two chains cannot do it: B, C and E are pairwise incomparable, so each needs its own chain.

15
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Dilworth: the largest antichain and the smallest chain cover have the same size. That size is the width.

16
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Width three, height three, three chains, three antichains. Both theorems hold on this poset, and they are different theorems.

17
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:16.000
The Boolean lattice of a three-element set: eight subsets, ordered by inclusion.

18
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:25.000
An antichain here is a family of sets no one of which contains another. The middle level has three such sets.

19
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Sperner: the widest level of the power set of an n-element set has C of n, floor of n over two members.

20
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:42.000
Antichains and maximum antichains are different counts: twenty antichains in this lattice, but only two of size three.

21
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:51.000
Twelve divisors of 60, ordered by divisibility: 4 divides 12, 12 divides 60, and 4 does not divide 6.

22
00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The width is four, and the page finds it by testing all 4096 subsets: 4, 6, 10, 15.

23
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
The height is five, from the layers 1; 2,3,5; 4,6,10,15; 12,20,30; 60.

24
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Four chains cover it: 1,2,4,12,60 and 3,6,30 and 5,10,20 and 15. Dilworth predicted four.

25
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:20.000
To build the cover, split every element into a left copy and a right copy, and draw an edge for each strict comparison.

26
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:28.000
A maximum matching pairs each left copy with at most one right copy. Here it has size three.

27
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.000
The matching turns into chains: follow the links, and every element used as a link is not a chain start.

28
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:44.000
So the width is computable: build the graph, find a maximum matching, subtract from n. That is the exam-safe route.

29
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:52.000
A seven-element poset with ranks 0, 1 and 2: one element, then three, then three. Every maximal chain has length three.

30
00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:01.000
The widest level has three elements. The width is four, and the maximum antichain is 1, 2, 5, 6.

31
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:08.000
What the levels do give is Mirsky: three antichains covering everything, matching the height of three.

32
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.000
The safe move: prove a lower bound with an antichain you exhibit, and an upper bound with chains you exhibit.

33
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Width and height are different numbers. Quoting Dilworth as "width equals height" is wrong.

34
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:28.000
A maximal antichain is not a maximum one. Maximal means you cannot add an element; maximum means nothing bigger exists.

35
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Greedy chain covers overpay: this seven-element poset has width four, and taking the longest chain first costs five chains.

36
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:44.000
In a general graph, independence number and clique cover need not agree. In a poset they do, because comparability graphs are perfect.

37
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:52.000
GATE CSE 1991, Q1(xiv): if the longest chain has length n, the poset can be written as a ____ of n antichains.

38
00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Why partition and not cover: the levels are disjoint, and n is the height, which no smaller number of antichains can beat.

39
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.000
GATE IT 2005, Q33: the largest family of subsets of an n-set that is totally ordered by inclusion has n plus one members.

40
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Chain questions give a longest chain or a widest antichain; read which, then quote Mirsky or Dilworth, never both at once.

41
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Step one: decide which number the question wants. Width, height, a chain cover, or a count of antichains.

42
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:28.000
Step two: exhibit a candidate antichain for the lower bound, and candidate chains for the upper bound.

43
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:36.000
Step three: if the poset is big, use the split graph and a maximum matching. Width equals n minus the matching size.

